A line $L$ in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through point $P_0(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ and is parallel to vector $\vec{v} = \langle a, b, c \rangle$ has \textbf{vector equation}:
\[
\boxed{\vec{r}(t) = \vec{r}_0 + t\vec{v}}
\]

where $\vec{r}_0 = \langle x_0, y_0, z_0 \rangle$ is the position vector of $P_0$.

Equivalently:
\[
\boxed{\vec{r}(t) = \langle x_0, y_0, z_0 \rangle + t\langle a, b, c \rangle}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Components:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\vec{r}_0$: position vector of a point on the line
    \item $\vec{v}$: direction vector (parallel to the line)
    \item $t$: parameter ($-\infty < t < \infty$)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Note:}}

Different values of $t$ give different points on the line:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $t = 0$: gives point $P_0$
    \item $t > 0$: points in direction of $\vec{v}$
    \item $t < 0$: points in opposite direction
\end{itemize}

