\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find parametric equations for the line through $P(1, 2, -1)$ parallel to $\vec{v} = \langle 3, -2, 4 \rangle$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Given:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Point: $(x_0, y_0, z_0) = (1, 2, -1)$
    \item Direction: $\langle a, b, c \rangle = \langle 3, -2, 4 \rangle$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Parametric equations:}
\[
\begin{cases}
x = 1 + 3t \\
y = 2 - 2t \\
z = -1 + 4t
\end{cases}
\]

\textbf{Vector form:}
\[
\vec{r}(t) = \langle 1, 2, -1 \rangle + t\langle 3, -2, 4 \rangle
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\begin{cases}
x = 1 + 3t \\
y = 2 - 2t \\
z = -1 + 4t
\end{cases}}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $a \neq 0$, $b \neq 0$, and $c \neq 0$, we can solve the parametric equations for $t$ and equate:
\[
\boxed{\frac{x - x_0}{a} = \frac{y - y_0}{b} = \frac{z - z_0}{c}}
\]

These are the \textbf{symmetric equations} of the line.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Special Cases:}}

If one direction component is zero (e.g., $a = 0$), write:
\[
x = x_0, \quad \frac{y - y_0}{b} = \frac{z - z_0}{c}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Advantage:}}

Symmetric form doesn't explicitly show the parameter $t$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Disadvantage:}}

Cannot be used if any direction component is zero (without modification).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find parametric and symmetric equations for the line through $P(2, 1, 0)$ and $Q(4, -1, 3)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find direction vector

\[
\vec{v} = \overrightarrow{PQ} = \langle 4-2, -1-1, 3-0 \rangle = \langle 2, -2, 3 \rangle
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Use point $P$ and direction $\vec{v}$ for parametric equations

\[
\begin{cases}
x = 2 + 2t \\
y = 1 - 2t \\
z = 0 + 3t
\end{cases}
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Convert to symmetric form

Solve each equation for $t$:
\begin{align*}
t &= \frac{x - 2}{2} \\
t &= \frac{y - 1}{-2} \\
t &= \frac{z - 0}{3}
\end{align*}

Equate:
\[
\frac{x - 2}{2} = \frac{y - 1}{-2} = \frac{z}{3}
\]

\textbf{Answer:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Parametric: \boxed{\begin{cases}
    x = 2 + 2t \\
    y = 1 - 2t \\
    z = 3t
    \end{cases}}
    \item Symmetric: \boxed{\frac{x - 2}{2} = \frac{y - 1}{-2} = \frac{z}{3}}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Determine if the lines are parallel:
\begin{align*}
L_1: \quad & x = 1 + 2t, \quad y = -1 + 3t, \quad z = 2 - t \\
L_2: \quad & x = 3 - 4s, \quad y = 2 - 6s, \quad z = 1 + 2s
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify direction vectors

\begin{align*}
\vec{v}_1 &= \langle 2, 3, -1 \rangle \\
\vec{v}_2 &= \langle -4, -6, 2 \rangle
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Check if one is a scalar multiple of the other

\[
\vec{v}_2 = -2\vec{v}_1 = -2\langle 2, 3, -1 \rangle = \langle -4, -6, 2 \rangle \quad \checkmark
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{Yes, the lines are parallel}}

Since $\vec{v}_2 = -2\vec{v}_1$, the direction vectors are parallel.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Determine if the lines intersect, and if so, find the point of intersection:
\begin{align*}
L_1: \quad & x = 1 + t, \quad y = 2 + 2t, \quad z = 1 - t \\
L_2: \quad & x = 3 - s, \quad y = 1 + s, \quad z = s
\end{align*}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set coordinates equal (different parameters!)

\begin{align*}
1 + t &= 3 - s \quad \text{(x-coordinates)} \\
2 + 2t &= 1 + s \quad \text{(y-coordinates)} \\
1 - t &= s \quad \text{(z-coordinates)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Solve the system

From equation 3: $s = 1 - t$

Substitute into equation 1:
\begin{align*}
1 + t &= 3 - (1 - t) \\
1 + t &= 2 + t \\
1 &= 2 \quad \text{(contradiction!)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\text{The lines do not intersect (they are skew)}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} Since we got a contradiction, the lines are skew (they don't meet and aren't parallel).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 6.7 Summary: Lines in $\mathbb{R}^3$}}

\textbf{Vector Form:}
\[
\boxed{\vec{r}(t) = \vec{r}_0 + t\vec{v}}
\]

\textbf{Parametric Form:}
\[
\boxed{\begin{cases}
x = x_0 + at \\
y = y_0 + bt \\
z = z_0 + ct
\end{cases}}
\]

\textbf{Symmetric Form:}
\[
\boxed{\frac{x - x_0}{a} = \frac{y - y_0}{b} = \frac{z - z_0}{c}}
\]

(provided $a, b, c \neq 0$)

\textbf{Line Through Two Points:}

Direction vector: $\vec{v} = \overrightarrow{PQ} = \langle x_2 - x_1, y_2 - y_1, z_2 - z_1 \rangle$

\textbf{Parallel Lines:} $\vec{v}_1 = k\vec{v}_2$

\textbf{Perpendicular Lines:} $\vec{v}_1 \cdot \vec{v}_2 = 0$

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Using the same parameter for both lines when testing intersection
    \item Forgetting to check all three coordinate equations
    \item Trying to use symmetric form when a direction component is zero
    \item Concluding lines don't intersect when they're actually parallel
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Testing Intersection:}} Set parametric equations equal (use different parameters!) and solve. If solution exists and satisfies all three equations, lines intersect.

