A plane in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that contains point $P_0(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ and has \textbf{normal vector} $\vec{n} = \langle a, b, c \rangle$ has equation:

\textbf{Vector Form:}
\[
\boxed{\vec{n} \cdot (\vec{r} - \vec{r}_0) = 0}
\]

\textbf{Scalar Form (Standard Equation):}
\[
\boxed{a(x - x_0) + b(y - y_0) + c(z - z_0) = 0}
\]

\textbf{General Form:}
\[
\boxed{ax + by + cz + d = 0}
\]

where $d = -(ax_0 + by_0 + cz_0)$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Key Concept:}}

The normal vector $\vec{n} = \langle a, b, c \rangle$ is perpendicular to every vector in the plane.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Derivation:}}

If $P(x, y, z)$ is any point on the plane, then $\overrightarrow{P_0P}$ lies in the plane, so:
\[
\vec{n} \cdot \overrightarrow{P_0P} = 0
\]

