\textbf{Cylindrical coordinates} $(r, \theta, z)$ of a point $P$ in $\mathbb{R}^3$ are defined as:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $r$: distance from $P$ to the $z$-axis (radial distance) $(r \geq 0)$
    \item $\theta$: angle in the $xy$-plane from the positive $x$-axis $(0 \leq \theta < 2\pi)$
    \item $z$: height above the $xy$-plane (same as Cartesian $z$)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Conversion from Cylindrical to Cartesian:}
\[
\boxed{\begin{cases}
x = r\cos\theta \\
y = r\sin\theta \\
z = z
\end{cases}}
\]

\textbf{Conversion from Cartesian to Cylindrical:}
\[
\boxed{\begin{cases}
r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\
\theta = \arctan\left(\frac{y}{x}\right) \\
z = z
\end{cases}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Interpretation:}}

Cylindrical coordinates extend polar coordinates $(r, \theta)$ from 2D to 3D by adding the $z$-coordinate.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{When to Use:}}

Cylindrical coordinates are ideal for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Cylinders
    \item Surfaces with circular cross-sections
    \item Problems with rotational symmetry about the $z$-axis
\end{itemize}

